{"task_id": "HumanEval/87", "solution": "def get_row(lst, x):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds integers x in the list and returns a list of tuples,\n    [(x1, y1), (x2, y2) ...] such that each tuple is a coordinate\n    (row, columns), starting with 0.\n    Sorts coordinates initially by rows in ascending order.\n    Also, sorts coordinates of the row by columns in descending order.\n    \"\"\"\n    found_coordinates = []\n\n    for row_idx, row_list in enumerate(lst):\n        for col_idx, value in enumerate(row_list):\n            if value == x:\n                found_coordinates.append((row_idx, col_idx))\n\n    # Sort the list of (row, col) tuples.\n    # Primary sort by row (ascending), secondary sort by column (descending).\n    # This is achieved by sorting by (row, -col) in ascending order.\n    found_coordinates.sort(key=lambda coord: (coord[0], -coord[1]))\n\n    return found_coordinates"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/87", "solution": "```python\ndef get_row(lst, x):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds integers x in a 2D list and returns their 0-indexed (row, column) coordinates.\n    Coordinates are sorted by row ascending, then by column descending.\n    \"\"\"\n    coordinates = []\n    for row_idx, row_list in enumerate(lst):\n        for col_idx, value in enumerate(row_list):\n            if value == x:\n                coordinates.append((row_idx, col_idx))\n\n    # Sort coordinates primarily by row (ascending) and secondarily by column (descending).\n    # Using -coord[1] effectively sorts columns in descending order.\n    coordinates.sort(key=lambda coord: (coord[0], -coord[1]))\n\n    return coordinates\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/88", "solution": "```python\ndef sort_array(array):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given an array of non-negative integers, return a copy of the given array after sorting,\n    you will sort the given array in ascending order if the sum( first index value, last index value) is odd,\n    or sort it in descending order if the sum( first index value, last index value) is even.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    copied_array = array[:] # Create a shallow copy to ensure the original array is not modified.\n\n    if len(copied_array) <= 1: # Handle empty or single-element arrays as edge cases.\n        return copied_array\n\n    first_element = copied_array[0]\n    last_element = copied_array[-1]\n    \n    if (first_element + last_element) % 2 != 0:  # Odd sum, sort in ascending order.\n        copied_array.sort()\n    else:  # Even sum, sort in descending order.\n        copied_array.sort(reverse=True)\n\n    return copied_array\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/88", "solution": "def sort_array(array):\n    \"\"\"\n    Sorts a copy of the given array based on the parity of its first and last elements' sum.\n    The original array remains unchanged.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not array:\n        return []\n\n    copied_array = list(array)\n\n    if len(copied_array) == 1:\n        return copied_array\n\n    sum_first_last = copied_array[0] + copied_array[-1]\n\n    if sum_first_last % 2 != 0:  # Sum is odd, sort ascending\n        copied_array.sort()\n    else:  # Sum is even, sort descending\n        copied_array.sort(reverse=True)\n\n    return copied_array"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/89", "solution": "LOWERCASE_ALPHABET = \"abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz\"\nUPPERCASE_ALPHABET = \"ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ\"\nSHIFT_AMOUNT = 4\n\ndef encrypt(s):\n    \"\"\"Encrypts a string by rotating alphabetic characters by 4 places.\n    Non-alphabetic characters remain unchanged, and case is preserved.\"\"\"\n    encrypted_chars = []\n    for char in s:\n        if char.islower():\n            original_index = LOWERCASE_ALPHABET.index(char)\n            new_index = (original_index + SHIFT_AMOUNT) % len(LOWERCASE_ALPHABET)\n            encrypted_chars.append(LOWERCASE_ALPHABET[new_index])\n        elif char.isupper():\n            original_index = UPPERCASE_ALPHABET.index(char)\n            new_index = (original_index + SHIFT_AMOUNT) % len(UPPERCASE_ALPHABET)\n            encrypted_chars.append(UPPERCASE_ALPHABET[new_index])\n        else:\n            encrypted_chars.append(char)\n    return \"\".join(encrypted_chars)"}
