{"task_id": "HumanEval/123", "solution": "def get_odd_collatz(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a positive integer n, return a sorted list that has the odd numbers in collatz sequence.\n    The Collatz conjecture is a conjecture in mathematics that concerns a sequence defined\n    as follows: start with any positive integer n. Then each term is obtained from the \n    previous term as follows: if the previous term is even, the next term is one half of \n    the previous term. If the previous term is odd, the next term is 3 times the previous\n    term plus 1. The conjecture is that no matter what value of n, the sequence will always reach 1.\n    \"\"\"\n    collatz_sequence = []\n    current = n\n    while True:\n        collatz_sequence.append(current)\n        if current == 1:\n            break\n        if current % 2 == 0:\n            current //= 2\n        else:\n            current = 3 * current + 1\n            \n    odd_numbers = {num for num in collatz_sequence if num % 2 != 0}\n    return sorted(list(odd_numbers))"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/123", "solution": "def get_odd_collatz(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a positive integer n, return a sorted list that has the odd numbers in collatz sequence.\n    The returned list contains unique odd numbers in increasing order.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n == 1:\n        return [1]\n\n    sequence = [n]\n    current_number = n\n    while current_number != 1:\n        if current_number % 2 == 0:\n            current_number //= 2\n        else:\n            current_number = 3 * current_number + 1\n        sequence.append(current_number)\n\n    odd_numbers = sorted(list(set(num for num in sequence if num % 2 != 0)))\n    return odd_numbers"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/125", "solution": "```python\nimport re\n\ndef split_words(txt):\n    '''\n    Given a string of words, return a list of words split on whitespace, if no whitespaces exists in the text you\n    should split on commas ',' if no commas exists you should return the number of lower-case letters with odd order in the\n    alphabet, ord('a') = 0, ord('b') = 1, ... ord('z') = 25\n    Examples\n    split_words(\"Hello world!\") \u279e [\"Hello\", \"world!\"]\n    split_words(\"Hello,world!\") \u279e [\"Hello\", \"world!\"]\n    split_words(\"abcdef\") == 3\n    '''\n    if re.search(r'\\s', txt):\n        return txt.split()\n    elif ',' in txt:\n        return txt.split(',')\n    else:\n        count = 0\n        for char in txt:\n            if 'a' <= char <= 'z':\n                order = ord(char) - ord('a')\n                if order % 2 != 0:\n                    count += 1\n        return count\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/125", "solution": "```python\ndef split_words(txt):\n    \"\"\"\n    Splits text by whitespace, then by commas, or counts odd-ordered lowercase letters.\n    Alphabetical order starts with 'a' at 0.\n    \"\"\"\n    if any(c.isspace() for c in txt):\n        return txt.split()\n    elif ',' in txt:\n        return txt.split(',')\n    else:\n        count = 0\n        for char in txt:\n            if 'a' <= char <= 'z':\n                order = ord(char) - ord('a')\n                if order % 2 != 0:\n                    count += 1\n        return count\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/124", "solution": "import re\n\ndef valid_date(date):\n    \"\"\"\n    Validates a given date string according to specified rules.\n    Returns True if the date is valid, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not date:\n        return False\n\n    # Regex to enforce \"mm-dd-yyyy\" format and initial range checks for month and day.\n    # Month: 01-12, Day: 01-31, Year: 19xx or 20xx.\n    DATE_FORMAT_REGEX = re.compile(r\"^(0[1-9]|1[0-2])-(0[1-9]|[12][0-9]|3[01])-(19|20)\\d{2}$\")\n    match = DATE_FORMAT_REGEX.match(date)\n\n    if not match:\n        return False\n\n    month_str, day_str, _ = match.groups()\n    month = int(month_str)\n    day = int(day_str)\n\n    # Defines the maximum days for each month, adhering to the 29-day limit for February.\n    DAYS_IN_MONTH = {\n        1: 31, 3: 31, 5: 31, 7: 31, 8: 31, 10: 31, 12: 31,\n        4: 30, 6: 30, 9: 30, 11: 30,\n        2: 29\n    }\n\n    max_days = DAYS_IN_MONTH.get(month)\n\n    # Check if the day is within the valid range for the identified month.\n    if not (1 <= day <= max_days):\n        return False\n\n    return True"}
